2383	/****************************************************************************
2384	 * Name: sunxi_intr_setup
2385	 *
2386	 * Description:
2387	 *   Process a IN or OUT request on any interrupt endpoint by inserting a qTD
2388	 *   into the periodic frame list.
2389	 *
2390	 *  Paragraph 4.10.7 "Adding Interrupt Queue Heads to the Periodic Schedule"
2391	 *    "The link path(s) from the periodic frame list to a queue head
2392	 *     establishes in which frames a transaction can be executed for the
2393	 *     queue head. Queue heads are linked into the periodic schedule so they
2394	 *     are polled at the appropriate rate. System software sets a bit in a
2395	 *     queue head's S-Mask to indicate which micro-frame with-in a 1
2396	 *     millisecond period a transaction should be executed for the queue
2397	 *     head. Software must ensure that all queue heads in the periodic
2398	 *     schedule have S-Mask set to a non-zero value. An S-mask with a zero
2399	 *     value in the context of the periodic schedule yields undefined
2400	 *     results.
2401	 *
2402	 *    "If the desired poll rate is greater than one frame, system software
2403	 *     can use a combination of queue head linking and S-Mask values to
2404	 *     spread interrupts of equal poll rates through the schedule so that the
2405	 *     periodic bandwidth is allocated and managed in the most efficient
2406	 *     manner possible."
2407	 *
2408	 *  Paragraph 4.6 "Periodic Schedule"
2409	 *
2410	 *    "The periodic schedule is used to manage all isochronous and interrupt
2411	 *     transfer streams. The base of the periodic schedule is the periodic
2412	 *     frame list. Software links schedule data structures to the periodic
2413	 *     frame list to produce a graph of scheduled data structures. The graph
2414	 *     represents an appropriate sequence of transactions on the USB. ...
2415	 *     isochronous transfers (using iTDs and siTDs) with a period of one are
2416	 *     linked directly to the periodic frame list. Interrupt transfers (are
2417	 *     managed with queue heads) and isochronous streams with periods other
2418	 *     than one are linked following the period-one iTD/siTDs. Interrupt
2419	 *     queue heads are linked into the frame list ordered by poll rate.
2420	 *     Longer poll rates are linked first (e.g. closest to the periodic
2421	 *     frame list), followed by shorter poll rates, with queue heads with a
2422	 *     poll rate of one, on the very end."
2423	 *
2424	 * Assumption:  The caller holds the EHCI lock.
2425	 *
2426	 * Returned Value:
2427	 *   Zero (OK) is returned on success; a negated errno value is return on
2428	 *   any failure.
2429	 *
2430	 ****************************************************************************/
